## Types of Embeddings

### Representation Models
**Definition:** Models that convert textual input to embeddings (e.g., Word2Vec)

### Tokenization in Modern Models

**Example Input:** "Her vocalization was melodic."

**Tokenization Process:**
- **Not** just splitting by whitespace
- "vocalization" → "vocal" + "ization"
- **Reason:** Tokenizers have fixed vocabulary
- Cannot represent all words individually
- Must find combinations when needed

### Building Different Embedding Types

#### Token Embeddings
- One embedding per token
- Direct output from model

#### Word Embeddings
- Created by averaging token embeddings
- Example: "vocalization" = average("vocal", "ization")
- Represents entire word

#### Sentence Embeddings
- Similar averaging/aggregation techniques
- Represents entire sentence

#### Document Embeddings
- Longer text aggregation
- Represents entire document

**Note:** Many other types of embeddings exist beyond these examples

---

## Key Limitations of Word2Vec

### Static Representations
Word2Vec creates **static** representations - the same word always has the same embedding regardless of context

**Preview:** Next lesson covers encoding/decoding **contextualized** information to address this limitation

